4. Initialize `i = 0` (index in available), `count = 0`.
5. While `cur` is less than or equal to the end of the last target (i.e., we haven't covered all targets):
    a. Among all available intervals that start <= `cur`, find the one with the maximum end. Let `max_end` be that end.
    b. If no such interval exists (i.e., the next available interval starts > `cur`), or `max_end < cur` (shouldn't happen if we have start <= cur), then return -1.
