Now, what about the case where available intervals might not be used in order? The algorithm assumes we process available intervals in sorted order and maintain a global pointer `i`. Is it possible that we need to "go back" to an interval we already passed? In the standard greedy for covering a single interval, we sort by start and then the pointer only moves forward. For multiple targets, since we always move `cur` to the right, and available is sorted by start, any interval we skip (because its start > cur at that time) will have start > current cur. Since cur only increases, we will never need an interval with start smaller than the current cur for the remaining coverage. But what about intervals that we skipped earlier that have start <= new cur? The inner while loop condition `avail[i][0] <= cur` will include them if `i` hasn't passed them. But `i` only advances when we process the inner while. After we finish an inner while, `i` points to the first interval with start > old cur. Then we set `cur = max_end`. Now new cur >= old cur. The next inner while will continue from the current `i`, and will include any intervals with start between old cur and new cur, because their start <= new cur and `i` is at the first interval with start > old cur, so those intervals are at positions >= i. So it works.

But there's a subtle case: what if we have an interval that starts very early, ends early, and we skip it because at the time cur was small, its end was < cur? Actually, if its end < cur, it means it doesn't cover cur. But could it be useful later? If it ends early, its end is less than the current cur, which is moving right. Since cur only increases, an interval that ended before the current cur will definitely end before any future cur (which is >= current cur). So it will never be useful. What if an interval starts early, ends after cur, but we didn't pick it because there was another interval with even larger end? We pick the one with max end, so we pick the best one. The ones we skip (because their end < max_end) are strictly worse and won't be needed.

What about intervals that start after the current cur but before some later target's start? They will be considered when cur advances past their start.

I think the algorithm is correct.

